Consider three processes $\text{P1, P2}$, and $\text{P3}$ running identical code, as shown in the pseudocode below. $\text{A}$ and $\text{B}$ are two binary semaphores initialized to $1$ and $0$, respectively. $\text{X}$ is a shared variable initialized to $0$. Each line in the pseudocode is executed atomically.<br><br>Pseudocode of P1, P2, and P3:<br><br>```
Wait(A);
Print(*);
X = X + 1;
If (X == 2) {
    Print($);
    Signal(B);
}
Signal(A);
Wait(B);
Print(#);
Signal(B);
```<br><br>Assume that any of the three processes can start to execute first and context switching can happen between these processes at any arbitrary time and in any arbitrary order.<br><br>Which of the following patterns is/are possible to be generated as an outcome of the execution of these three processes?

answer_text:
The problem asks: three processes P1, P2, P3 execute identical code with binary semaphores <code>A</code> (initialized to <code>1</code>) and <code>B</code> (initialized to <code>0</code>), and shared integer <code>X</code> (initialized to <code>0</code>). We must determine which output patterns (strings of <code>*</code>, <code>#</code>, and a single <code>$</code>) are possible under arbitrary interleaving.<br><br><strong>Key Concepts:</strong><br><br>1. <strong>Semaphore <code>A</code></strong> ensures that only one process at a time enters the critical section that prints <code>*</code> and increments <code>X</code>.<br><br>2. <strong>Semaphore <code>B</code></strong> is initially <code>0</code>, so any process that reaches <code>Wait(B)</code> will block until another process executes <code>Signal(B)</code>.<br><br>3. <strong>Variable <code>X</code></strong> counts how many processes have executed <code>X = X + 1</code>. The second process to increment <code>X</code> (making <code>X = 2</code>) prints the single <code>$</code> and signals <code>B</code> (making <code>B = 1</code>), allowing one blocked process to proceed.<br><br>4. After <code>Signal(A)</code>, a process moves to <code>Wait(B)</code>. Once it passes <code>Wait(B)</code>, it prints <code>#</code> and signals <code>B</code> again, releasing the next waiting process.<br><br><strong>Step-by-step reasoning:</strong><br><br><strong>Step 1: Understand the code behavior.</strong><br><br>Each process executes atomically:<br><br>```
Wait(A);
Print(*);
X = X + 1;
If (X == 2) {
    Print($);
    Signal(B);
}
Signal(A);
Wait(B);
Print(#);
Signal(B);
```<br><br><strong>Step 2: Key observations.</strong><br><br><ul><li>All three <code>*</code> are always printed (one per process).</li><li>Exactly one <code>$</code> is printed, by the second process that increments <code>X</code>.</li><li>All three <code>#</code> are printed, but the first <code>#</code> can only occur after the <code>$</code> (because <code>B</code> starts at <code>0</code>).</li><li>The order of <code>*</code>, <code>$</code>, <code>#</code> can vary except that <code>$</code> cannot appear after the third <code>*</code>.</li></ul><br><br><strong>Step 3: Evaluate each option.</strong><br><br><strong>Option A: <code>**$*###</code></strong><br><br>Sequence: <code>* * $ * # # #</code>.<br><br>Possible? Yes. Example interleaving:<br><br>1. P1: <code>*</code>, <code>X = 1</code>.<br><br>2. P2: <code>*</code>, <code>X = 2</code> → prints <code>$</code>, signals <code>B</code> (<code>B = 1</code>).<br><br>3. P3: <code>*</code>, <code>X = 3</code>.<br><br>4. All three at <code>Wait(B)</code>. P1 gets <code>B = 1</code>: prints <code>#</code>, signals <code>B</code> (<code>B = 1</code>).<br><br>5. P2 prints <code>#</code>, signals <code>B</code>.<br><br>6. P3 prints <code>#</code>, signals <code>B</code>.<br><br>Output: <code>**$*###</code>. (Possible)<br><br><strong>Option B: <code>**$#*##</code></strong><br><br>Sequence: <code>* * $ # * # #</code>.<br><br>Possible? Yes. Example:<br><br>1. P1: <code>*</code>, <code>X = 1</code>.<br><br>2. P2: <code>*</code>, <code>X = 2</code> → prints <code>$</code>, signals <code>B</code> (<code>B = 1</code>).<br><br>3. P1 (at <code>Wait(B)</code>) gets <code>B = 1</code>: prints <code>#</code>, signals <code>B</code>.<br><br>4. P2 (at <code>Wait(B)</code>) prints <code>#</code>, signals <code>B</code>.<br><br>5. P3 enters: <code>*</code>, <code>X = 3</code>, then at <code>Wait(B)</code> gets <code>B = 1</code>: prints <code>#</code>.<br><br>Output: <code>**$#*##</code>. (Possible)<br><br><strong>Option C: <code>**$##*#</code></strong><br><br>Sequence: <code>* * $ # # * #</code>.<br><br>Possible? Yes. Example:<br><br>1. P1: <code>*</code>, <code>X = 1</code>.<br><br>2. P2: <code>*</code>, <code>X = 2</code> → prints <code>$</code>, signals <code>B</code> (<code>B = 1</code>).<br><br>3. P1 prints <code>#</code>, signals <code>B</code>.<br><br>4. P3 enters: <code>*</code>, <code>X = 3</code>, blocks at <code>Wait(B)</code>.<br><br>5. P2 prints <code>#</code>, signals <code>B</code>.<br><br>6. P3 prints <code>#</code>.<br><br>Output: <code>**$##*#</code>. (Possible)<br><br><strong>Option D: <code>***$###</code></strong><br><br>Sequence: <code>* * * $ # # #</code>.<br><br>Impossible? Yes. Why? To print the third <code>*</code>, the third process must enter the critical section. But <code>$</code> is printed by the second process when <code>X</code> becomes <code>2</code>. If the third process increments <code>X</code> to <code>3</code> before the second process checks <code>X == 2</code>, then the second process will see <code>X = 3</code> and will not print <code>$</code>. Therefore, <code>$</code> cannot appear after the third <code>*</code>. (Impossible)<br><br><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Options A, B, and C are possible. Option D is impossible.<br><br>The correct answer is <code>A, B, C</code>.

